00:01:11.169 --> 00:01:12.274
- Okay, so my question is,

00:01:12.274 --> 00:01:13.336
so you know how Facebook reach

00:01:13.336 --> 00:01:14.511
has been going down.

00:01:14.511 --> 00:01:15.244
- Organic.

00:01:15.244 --> 00:01:17.426
- And this is the lowest
levels we've ever seen.

00:01:17.426 --> 00:01:18.204
- Yep.

00:01:18.204 --> 00:01:21.304
- So, I'm curious, what do
you think is the role of

00:01:21.304 --> 00:01:23.289
organic or unpaid content where

00:01:23.289 --> 00:01:25.784
the brand is always on
strategy, and the second part--

00:01:25.784 --> 00:01:27.142
- Within a Facebook world?

00:01:27.142 --> 00:01:28.210
- Within the Facebook world,

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:29.441
and the second part of the question is,

00:01:29.441 --> 00:01:32.088
just how much reach is
enough to actually justify

00:01:32.088 --> 00:01:34.166
the time, effort, and
resources that go into

00:01:34.166 --> 00:01:35.525
producing these assets?

00:01:35.525 --> 00:01:36.802
- That's a great question, man.

00:01:36.802 --> 00:01:37.962
Nice start.

00:01:37.962 --> 00:01:41.188
Um, you know I think it all depends on

00:01:41.188 --> 00:01:43.186
size, scale, and objective, right.

00:01:43.186 --> 00:01:45.264
So I think the biggest problem
that everybody makes is

00:01:45.264 --> 00:01:47.423
there's no one size that fits all.

00:01:47.423 --> 00:01:49.339
Obviously, the brands
that we work with here

00:01:49.339 --> 00:01:51.301
are at huge scale,

00:01:51.301 --> 00:01:53.100
versus let's say a lot of people watching

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:54.586
who've got a small business.

00:01:54.586 --> 00:01:57.164
You know, we manage some brand pages

00:01:57.164 --> 00:01:58.626
that I can think of right now,

00:01:58.626 --> 00:02:00.960
that are so large in overall size

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:03.327
and have done a good job
putting out great content

00:02:03.327 --> 00:02:05.568
that they're still getting
hundreds of thousands

00:02:05.568 --> 00:02:09.042
of impressions organically
without paid up front.

00:02:09.042 --> 00:02:11.085
Now obviously, all of you have heard me

00:02:11.085 --> 00:02:14.185
ranting about dark posts for quite a bit.

00:02:14.185 --> 00:02:16.065
And we even talked about this
when you were interviewing.

00:02:16.065 --> 00:02:19.061
So for me, you know,
do I feel that Facebook

00:02:19.061 --> 00:02:21.788
has evolved into a place
that you want to look at

00:02:21.788 --> 00:02:25.086
80, 90, even 100% of your posts

00:02:25.086 --> 00:02:27.569
are being preplanned to its audience

00:02:27.569 --> 00:02:29.021
and then paid upfront?

00:02:29.021 --> 00:02:30.762
You know, if you're a fortune 500 company

00:02:30.762 --> 00:02:32.759
I do believe that that justifies the case.

00:02:32.759 --> 00:02:34.326
And I believe that
because I actually think

00:02:34.326 --> 00:02:36.184
that those working media
dollars, those paid dollars,

00:02:36.184 --> 00:02:38.320
are a hell of a lot
better spent on Facebook,

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:41.025
than they are on traditional
banner or things of that nature

00:02:41.025 --> 00:02:43.021
places and organizations that you can from

00:02:43.021 --> 00:02:44.588
giving those kind of advices.

00:02:44.588 --> 00:02:47.723
So, I think that that's the case.

00:02:47.723 --> 00:02:49.778
Now, what's the threshold?

00:02:49.778 --> 00:02:51.659
I think that comes down
to the objective at hand.

00:02:51.659 --> 00:02:53.458
Look you can be a Fortune 500 Company,

00:02:53.458 --> 00:02:56.545
only reach 16 hundred people organically,

00:02:56.545 --> 00:02:59.379
but try to be selling
something that's $10,000

00:02:59.379 --> 00:03:00.923
as a B to B product

00:03:00.923 --> 00:03:02.605
and if you convert four people,

00:03:02.605 --> 00:03:04.939
and you're making $40,000 on it.

00:03:04.939 --> 00:03:07.238
You're profit margin is 50%

00:03:07.238 --> 00:03:08.898
and you've made $20,000 in profit,

00:03:08.898 --> 00:03:12.844
and your agency charged you $800 or $1800

00:03:12.844 --> 00:03:14.621
well then you justify the means.

00:03:14.621 --> 00:03:16.281
So I think it's, one of the biggest things

00:03:16.281 --> 00:03:17.311
that we try to do here,

00:03:17.311 --> 00:03:19.448
and one thing I think all of
you need to pay attention to

00:03:19.448 --> 00:03:22.152
is how do you become
efficient on the back end.

00:03:22.152 --> 00:03:23.406
I think what's separating us,

00:03:23.406 --> 00:03:24.823
and what I'm excited about here,

00:03:24.823 --> 00:03:28.130
is we're producing quality
content at a cost level

00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:30.221
that the market has
never seen before, right?

00:03:30.221 --> 00:03:31.811
And that's out advantage, right?

00:03:31.811 --> 00:03:33.029
That for you, with fresh eyes,

00:03:33.029 --> 00:03:34.528
is probably the difference
that you're seeing.

00:03:34.528 --> 00:03:36.489
That's what you guys have to think about.

00:03:36.489 --> 00:03:38.347
For a lot of entrepreneurs
that are watching,

00:03:38.347 --> 00:03:40.204
and I know that's a core 
of my audience, is

00:03:40.204 --> 00:03:42.410
is your time worth it.

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:44.244
Because it's not a money
game, it's a time game.

00:03:44.244 --> 00:03:45.648
So it's always resources.

00:03:45.648 --> 00:03:47.750
To me there is no one size fits all.

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:51.001
For all the brand managers,
and the CMOs and the CFOs

00:03:51.001 --> 00:03:52.567
and the corporates that
are watching the show

00:03:52.567 --> 00:03:53.868
or listening to the show,

00:03:53.868 --> 00:03:55.923
I know for a fact that they need to

00:03:55.923 --> 00:03:57.862
really look at just the math, right.

00:03:57.862 --> 00:03:59.766
Like, am I paying more
that what I'm reaching.

00:03:59.766 --> 00:04:02.679
So if you're paying a
traditional digital video shop

00:04:02.679 --> 00:04:04.827
$10,000 to make a video,

00:04:04.827 --> 00:04:06.162
and then you post it organically

00:04:06.162 --> 00:04:09.564
and it reaches 900
people, that's off, right.

00:04:09.564 --> 00:04:13.464
So, I just think that you have
to look at it case by case.

00:04:13.464 --> 00:04:14.485
- Yeah, yeah, that makes sense.

00:04:14.485 --> 00:04:15.020
Thank you.

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:15.937
- Thanks brother, welcome.

00:04:15.937 --> 00:04:16.680
- Thank you.

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:17.642
- Next.

00:04:17.642 --> 00:04:18.839
- I'm here with me.

00:04:18.839 --> 00:04:21.288
- I don't care, I'm changing the rules.

00:04:21.288 --> 00:04:22.287
Sorry Stunwin.

00:04:22.287 --> 00:04:23.390
- Always changing the rules.

00:04:23.390 --> 00:04:24.725
So my question for you--

00:04:24.725 --> 00:04:25.805
- What, what?

00:04:25.805 --> 00:04:27.024
- Okay, hi Gary.

00:04:27.024 --> 00:04:27.906
I'm Emily.

00:04:27.906 --> 00:04:29.676
- I know, not for me, for them.

00:04:29.676 --> 00:04:33.473
- I'm Emily McDonough, I
work in HR, Gary's team.

00:04:33.473 --> 00:04:35.295
- How long have you been at Vayner?

00:04:35.295 --> 00:04:36.397
- About three and a half years,

00:04:36.397 --> 00:04:37.451
and I've had some different
roles in the building.

00:04:37.451 --> 00:04:38.501
- OG
- So.

00:04:38.501 --> 00:04:39.524
- Really opposite play there.

00:04:39.524 --> 00:04:40.720
- Yeah, OG.

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:42.984
- Okay, see my order was better.

00:04:42.984 --> 00:04:45.944
For the story of OG, new beat, thank you.

00:04:45.944 --> 00:04:48.940
- So legend has it that one
your first date with your wife

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:51.215
you told her that you
we're going to marry her.

00:04:51.215 --> 00:04:53.525
