copywriter for VaynerMedia.

00:08:27.355 --> 00:08:29.444
I've been here about two
years, a little over now.

00:08:29.444 --> 00:08:30.815
- Jim, is that what you said?

00:08:30.815 --> 00:08:31.697
- James.

00:08:31.697 --> 00:08:32.602
No, I don't go by Jim.

00:08:32.602 --> 00:08:33.961
- I thought you said Jim,
and I like freaked out.

00:08:33.961 --> 00:08:35.342
I'm like is this some new guy?

00:08:35.342 --> 00:08:37.756
- It's a new James goin' into 2015.

00:08:37.756 --> 00:08:38.874
- That's good, I didn't hear you.

00:08:38.874 --> 00:08:41.892
- So, my question is this.

00:08:41.892 --> 00:08:44.958
When it comes to new
platforms, emerging tech

00:08:44.958 --> 00:08:47.894
you've always promoted the
idea of being a first mover.

00:08:47.894 --> 00:08:51.249
Most brands, some brands are
usually very receptive to that.

00:08:51.249 --> 00:08:52.736
When it comes to campaigns though,

00:08:52.736 --> 00:08:56.437
some brands tend to focus
on the idea of ownablity.

00:08:56.437 --> 00:08:57.854
My question is this--

00:08:57.854 --> 00:09:00.269
- Ownability as like, we need
to dominate this platform?

00:09:00.269 --> 00:09:02.650
- Or in terms of a campaign, when is that,

00:09:02.650 --> 00:09:06.607
how ownable is that campaign
to, you know, said brand?

00:09:06.607 --> 00:09:10.114
My question is this, when
does it become, at what point,

00:09:10.114 --> 00:09:13.712
a campaign ownable by being the
first brand to do something.

00:09:13.712 --> 00:09:16.290
As in being the first mover
to do that type of campaign?

00:09:16.290 --> 00:09:18.194
- You know, I think we've
see it in our space, right.

00:09:18.194 --> 00:09:21.711
Oreo kinda took control
of the real time content

00:09:21.711 --> 00:09:23.313
people keep bringing it up
over and over and here I am

00:09:23.313 --> 00:09:26.192
two years later bringing
it up and so I think

00:09:26.192 --> 00:09:29.628
you can get first mover
advantage in that way.

00:09:29.628 --> 00:09:32.786
Listen, and this is
gonna get me in trouble,

00:09:32.786 --> 00:09:35.074
I think that's a bunch of marketing

00:09:35.074 --> 00:09:35.886
(beep)

00:09:35.886 --> 00:09:38.543
When brands think they can own something

00:09:38.543 --> 00:09:41.785
it just speaks to their naivete
of the heavy fragmentation

00:09:41.785 --> 00:09:43.967
of the world we now live in, right.

00:09:43.967 --> 00:09:47.508
You can own something for
a moment, but to own it.

00:09:47.508 --> 00:09:49.412
Like, there's no brand that's gonna own,

00:09:49.412 --> 00:09:51.410
what brand owns what, right?

00:09:51.410 --> 00:09:54.391
I guess over thirty
years of iconic marketing

00:09:54.391 --> 00:09:58.410
Nike could own the sports
space, but do they?

00:09:58.410 --> 00:09:59.989
Because last time I checked,

00:09:59.989 --> 00:10:02.031
Under Armor is growing very quickly,

00:10:02.031 --> 00:10:04.029
and a million of other
things are happening

00:10:04.029 --> 00:10:06.233
in the world of soul cycle and crossfit.

00:10:06.233 --> 00:10:10.793
There is no ownable, this is
the naivete, the audacity,

00:10:10.793 --> 00:10:13.768
and just like the act of
contextual understanding

00:10:13.768 --> 00:10:16.787
that brands managers and
CMOs and corporate America

00:10:16.787 --> 00:10:20.502
think that they have the right
to be able own these things.

00:10:20.502 --> 00:10:22.578
I think here's what you
need to do as a brand

00:10:22.578 --> 00:10:24.040
from an ownable standpoint.

00:10:24.040 --> 00:10:25.979
Can you at this moment own it?

00:10:25.979 --> 00:10:28.137
That it matters enough to your customers

00:10:28.137 --> 00:10:30.320
to make them buy your shit, right.

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:32.120
And that's all you can ever ask for

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:35.452
and I think people are way
too romantic in marketing.

00:10:35.452 --> 00:10:37.239
And thinking that they're gonna create the

00:10:37.239 --> 00:10:40.641
Just Do It, or the Mastercard Price List,

00:10:40.641 --> 00:10:42.440
and it's gonna be this thing that they can

00:10:42.440 --> 00:10:44.715
put on their resume and
kinda can live off it

00:10:44.715 --> 00:10:46.632
for the rest of their lives.

00:10:46.632 --> 00:10:49.058
To me, it's much more about fragmentation.

00:10:49.058 --> 00:10:51.774
It's much more about being great

00:10:51.774 --> 00:10:53.538
at your last at bat every time.

00:10:53.538 --> 00:10:55.569
You know I always say you're only as good

00:10:55.569 --> 00:10:56.673
as your last at bat, right.

00:10:56.673 --> 00:10:58.076
This show doing well.

00:10:58.076 --> 00:11:00.074
If this episode sucks crap,

00:11:00.074 --> 00:11:01.618
it's heading in the wrong direction.

00:11:01.618 --> 00:11:05.473
And all 38 before it mean
nothing as I go forward.

00:11:05.473 --> 00:11:06.970
And so I think that's how marketers

00:11:06.970 --> 00:11:07.678
need to think about that,

00:11:07.678 --> 00:11:10.307
which is do the best you
can for what you need to do

00:11:10.307 --> 00:11:13.112
at that moment on the platforms
that you decide to storytell

00:11:13.112 --> 00:11:14.992
that actually drive business results.

00:11:14.992 --> 00:11:17.314
Not this romantic feeling
of, like, let's own it,

00:11:17.314 --> 00:11:19.589
marketing 360, fully integrated.

00:11:19.589 --> 00:11:20.808
I mean like all this jargon.

00:11:20.808 --> 00:11:22.933
Jargon, James,

00:11:22.933 --> 00:11:24.373
- Thank you.
- You're welcome.

00:11:24.373 --> 00:11:25.533
- I appreciate it.
- But don't say that to the client.

00:11:25.533 --> 00:11:27.745
- Obviously.
- Just kidding, say it.

00:11:27.745 --> 00:11:28.860
- [Voiceover] Video question.

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:30.022
- Oh, video question time?

00:11:30.022 --> 00:11:30.928
All right.

00:11:30.928 --> 00:11:32.666
- Hey Gary, it's Aimee from San Fransisco.

00:11:32.666 --> 00:11:33.555
- Hey, Aimee.

00:11:33.555 --> 00:11:34.935
- My question is the following.

00:11:34.935 --> 00:11:36.550
What's the most important
thing you've learned

00:11:36.550 --> 00:11:38.350
while growing your company at VaynerMedia

00:11:38.350 --> 00:11:39.696
from East Coast to West Coast,

00:11:39.696 --> 00:11:43.120
and how does a company successfully scale?

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:44.309
- You know, thanks Aimee.

00:11:44.309 --> 00:11:48.833
You know, it's funny, I never
opened a second Wine Library

00:11:48.833 --> 00:11:50.882
so opening a second
office in San Francisco

00:11:50.882 --> 00:11:53.691
and a third in L.A. has been
a new phenomenon for me.

00:11:53.691 --> 00:11:56.268
And quite honestly,
there's challenges in it.

00:11:56.268 --> 00:11:59.101
You know, obviously I
wanna be everywhere, right.

00:11:59.101 --> 00:12:00.947
And that matters so much, but look,

00:12:00.947 --> 00:12:02.143
even at VaynerMedia I'm not sure

00:12:02.143 --> 00:12:03.501
the last time I've been on the 15th floor.

00:12:03.501 --> 00:12:05.707
Everybody's asking for an
episode of the 15th floor.

00:12:05.707 --> 00:12:07.146
I don't remember, I haven't
been on the 15th floor

00:12:07.146 --> 00:12:09.120
in a week, in a month, excuse me.

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:11.581
It's a challenge when
you're one human being

00:12:11.581 --> 00:12:13.682
and so for me so much of it is high touch

00:12:13.682 --> 00:12:15.946
and kinda the way I wanna scale.

00:12:15.946 --> 00:12:17.559
Aimee, to answer your second part,

00:12:17.559 --> 00:12:18.801
how do you scale a company?

00:12:18.801 --> 00:12:20.821
I actually think you scale a company

00:12:20.821 --> 00:12:23.364
by doing unscalable things.

00:12:23.364 --> 00:12:25.488
Because I really believe you

00:12:25.488 --> 00:12:26.463
have to know your business.

00:12:26.463 --> 00:12:28.298
So at Wine Library I didn't
need a lot of people,

00:12:28.298 --> 00:12:29.806
and it was about selling wine.

00:12:29.806 --> 00:12:31.200
Thus it was a different company.

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:33.045
Here, we sell people.

00:12:33.045 --> 00:12:35.240
We sell our hours against a scope

00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:38.281
and our thinking and
so all I got is people.

00:12:38.281 --> 00:12:40.464
And so for me, scaling this company

00:12:40.464 --> 00:12:42.866
has been doing everything
that's unscalable.

00:12:42.866 --> 00:12:45.885
Which is sitting down
and mentoring one by one

00:12:45.885 --> 00:12:47.626
spending as much time as I can.

00:12:47.626 --> 00:12:50.215
And trying to empower
people to feel comfortable

00:12:50.215 --> 00:12:51.817
with coming to me.

00:12:51.817 --> 00:12:53.965
Now, starting to build
out an H.R. department

00:12:53.965 --> 00:12:55.219
after a nine month search.

00:12:55.219 --> 00:12:57.645
Finding Minnie and
saying, this is a person

00:12:57.645 --> 00:13:00.617
I'm willing to build and
has the natural nuisances

00:13:00.617 --> 00:13:04.077
to build the culture and the H.R. and E.Q.

00:13:04.077 --> 00:13:05.876
that I want for this organization.

00:13:05.876 --> 00:13:07.919
And then having people
that have been with you

00:13:07.919 --> 00:13:08.976
for three and a half years,

00:13:08.976 --> 00:13:10.462
you can show Emily
again, and you can wave,

00:13:10.462 --> 00:13:12.818
and so moving her into to H.R. department

00:13:12.818 --> 00:13:14.281
as you just heard earlier.

00:13:14.281 --> 00:13:17.160
So Aimee, for me scaling
this company because I know.

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:20.253
Look everybody who has a
business has to understand

00:13:20.253 --> 00:13:21.466
what business they're building.

00:13:21.466 --> 00:13:23.313
And no matter what you do,
it's always about people.

00:13:23.313 --> 00:13:25.298
When you're an agency or client services,

00:13:25.298 --> 00:13:27.678
it's extreme people which is so,

00:13:27.678 --> 00:13:30.034
for me the way to really scale it has been

00:13:30.034 --> 00:13:32.658
deeply entrenching myself into the people

00:13:32.658 --> 00:13:35.932
that work with me, for me, along side me,

00:13:35.932 --> 00:13:37.673
and that's very important to me.

00:13:37.673 --> 00:13:41.376
Um, the West Coast
offices dynamic, you know,

00:13:41.376 --> 00:13:44.220
Alena, if you're watching
more Skpye sessions.

00:13:44.220 --> 00:13:45.672
You know how I feel about that.

00:13:45.672 --> 00:13:47.610
It's more communication, communication is

00:13:47.610 --> 00:13:50.374
the backbone of this whole execution

00:13:50.374 --> 00:13:53.008
and so more time, more physical time.

00:13:53.008 --> 00:13:55.632
Something Lizzie and I are speaking about.

00:13:55.632 --> 00:13:57.072
How much time I'm gonna
spend on the West Coast

00:13:57.072 --> 00:13:58.836
in 15 and 16 is a big commitment to me.

00:13:58.836 --> 00:14:02.493
So just hacking, hacking away at

00:14:02.493 --> 00:14:03.735
the thing that matters the most

00:14:03.735 --> 00:14:07.090
which is do I have a
relationship with all 400 people.

00:14:07.090 --> 00:14:09.447
And when it's 4,000 people,
do I have a relationship

00:14:09.447 --> 00:14:11.211
with all 4,000 people.

00:14:11.211 --> 00:14:14.346
And I understand the
cynicism that one could have

00:14:14.346 --> 00:14:15.727
listening to that answer.

00:14:15.727 --> 00:14:17.271
Like how could you possibly have that?

00:14:17.271 --> 00:14:19.453
And the way you have it is by having

00:14:19.453 --> 00:14:21.567
a relationship with the first 40 people.

00:14:21.567 --> 00:14:23.691
Then having it with the next 400 people.

00:14:23.691 --> 00:14:25.815
Because the stunning amount of

00:14:25.815 --> 00:14:26.930
some of the people in this room,

00:14:26.930 --> 00:14:28.230
and some of the people outside this room,

00:14:28.230 --> 00:14:29.728
and in San Francisco and L.A.,

00:14:29.728 --> 00:14:31.294
the stunning amount of people that

00:14:31.294 --> 00:14:33.988
now help me scale, because when somebody

00:14:33.988 --> 00:14:37.192
struggling or screw this
place, or I don't believe Gary,

00:14:37.192 --> 00:14:39.944
they're quick to jump
in and tell 400 stories

00:14:39.944 --> 00:14:41.464
about why it's the other way

00:14:41.464 --> 00:14:43.310
which then gives that person the ammo

00:14:43.310 --> 00:14:45.998
to maybe jump in and
relook at the situation

00:14:45.998 --> 00:14:47.383
a different way, and that can.

00:14:47.383 --> 00:14:49.600
I always say the truth
is undefeated. Right?

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:54.033
And so, for me scaling it is by,

00:14:54.033 --> 00:14:55.586
by delivering for your teammates.

00:14:55.586 --> 00:14:56.491
- Good, how are you?

00:14:56.491 --> 00:14:57.548
- Tell the VaynerNation.

00:14:57.548 --> 00:14:59.324
- Hello VaynerNation, I'm Alex Davis.

00:14:59.324 --> 00:15:01.529
I work on new business and all things.

00:15:01.529 --> 00:15:03.585
I'm an account director here.

00:15:03.585 --> 00:15:05.988
I've been here, I don't
know, about eight months now.

00:15:05.988 --> 00:15:07.323
Feels like a year almost.

00:15:07.323 --> 00:15:09.261
I'm really excited for my Vaynerversary.

00:15:09.261 --> 00:15:10.086
(laughter)

00:15:10.086 --> 00:15:12.606
So I wanted to ask you a question

00:15:12.606 --> 00:15:13.777
which I think is going to help

00:15:13.777 --> 00:15:15.114
some of the entrepreneur viewers

00:15:15.114 --> 00:15:17.366
but also just the future
leaders of VaynerMedia.

00:15:17.366 --> 00:15:21.011
When you're transitioning
from that doer to a leader

00:15:21.011 --> 00:15:23.553
knowing what your priorities are

00:15:23.553 --> 00:15:25.654
and where to put your
focus, so that it counts.

00:15:25.654 --> 00:15:26.409
- Yeah.

00:15:26.409 --> 00:15:28.093
- Can you talk a little bit about that?

00:15:28.093 --> 00:15:29.010
- Yeah I can.

00:15:29.010 --> 00:15:30.647
And you know this is
something that I have a lot.

00:15:30.647 --> 00:15:32.933
And to put a little more
color for everybody listening,

00:15:32.933 --> 00:15:35.893
at Vayner and a lot of other
places you get into a place

00:15:35.893 --> 00:15:37.531
where you, I think you said it right,

00:15:37.531 --> 00:15:39.213
you're in execution mode and then

00:15:39.213 --> 00:15:40.734
all of a sudden you're managing a team.

00:15:40.734 --> 00:15:42.151
And those are two very different things.

00:15:42.151 --> 00:15:44.171
And it's the thing that I most fear

00:15:44.171 --> 00:15:46.434
in the organization, period, end of story.

00:15:46.434 --> 00:15:50.208
Because you have incredible
executors who yearn

00:15:50.208 --> 00:15:53.249
for the financial upside and the title

00:15:53.249 --> 00:15:55.943
to then lead a team and boy are those

00:15:55.943 --> 00:16:00.184
two very, very, very, very,
very, very, very, very, very,

00:16:00.184 --> 00:16:04.847
very, very, very, very
different skill sets.

00:16:04.847 --> 00:16:08.131
And so I think the biggest
thing people struggle with,

00:16:08.131 --> 00:16:09.414
there are so many things people

00:16:09.414 --> 00:16:10.633
struggle with with the transition.

00:16:10.633 --> 00:16:13.117
Number one, the thing I hate
more than anything in the world

00:16:13.117 --> 00:16:14.591
which is micromanagment.

00:16:14.591 --> 00:16:16.669
I wanna kick micromanagment in the face.

00:16:16.669 --> 00:16:17.818
I hate it.

00:16:17.818 --> 00:16:19.932
And, you know, it's something I just despise

00:16:19.932 --> 00:16:22.195
and it's a major struggle
because if you are

00:16:22.195 --> 00:16:23.995
a great executor you know how

00:16:23.995 --> 00:16:25.469
to cross those T's and dot those I's.

00:16:25.469 --> 00:16:27.176
And you know, when you see the person

00:16:27.176 --> 00:16:29.079
on your team whose not
as strong at executing

00:16:29.079 --> 00:16:31.822
you can't help it, it
goes against the grain.

00:16:31.832 --> 00:16:33.073
So that's number one thing.

00:16:33.073 --> 00:16:35.151
Facing yourself in the
mirror and understanding,

00:16:35.151 --> 00:16:36.869
and I've talked to you about this,

00:16:36.869 --> 00:16:38.157
and I've talked to so many leaders

00:16:38.157 --> 00:16:39.318
in this company about this.

00:16:39.318 --> 00:16:41.234
This is where people get pissed at me.

00:16:41.234 --> 00:16:42.650
Most things don't matter.

00:16:42.650 --> 00:16:45.877
And that's a very tough mental transition

00:16:45.877 --> 00:16:47.734
to somebody that manages a team.

00:16:47.734 --> 00:16:49.511
The other thing that a lot of
people will struggle with,

00:16:49.511 --> 00:16:52.575
and I talk about this quite a bit

00:16:52.575 --> 00:16:53.969
in this organization as well,

00:16:53.969 --> 00:16:56.291
is when you're a leader you have to be

00:16:56.291 --> 00:16:58.206
the bigger man and woman
in every situation.

00:16:58.206 --> 00:17:00.586
And a lot of times people,
especially when they

00:17:00.586 --> 00:17:02.153
make that first transition,

00:17:02.153 --> 00:17:03.814
and it's the first time
that they're the leader,

00:17:03.814 --> 00:17:07.075
they look at it wrongfully
because of society,

00:17:07.075 --> 00:17:10.569
as I'm the boss and they
try to impose their will

00:17:10.569 --> 00:17:13.809
instead of what I think
the real skill set is.

00:17:13.809 --> 00:17:16.013
Which is become a full time listener,

00:17:16.013 --> 00:17:19.509
a full time empowerer,
a full time eat crap

00:17:19.509 --> 00:17:22.944
and have humility and
empathy and self awareness.

00:17:22.944 --> 00:17:26.765
So you go from, what I
believe is I.Q to E. Q.

00:17:26.765 --> 00:17:28.993
and a lot of people can't
make that transition.

00:17:28.993 --> 00:17:31.385
I think the reason this
organization has grown so much

00:17:31.385 --> 00:17:35.133
is that's all I focus on
when that transition happens.

00:17:35.133 --> 00:17:37.027
And don't try to put pressure on people

00:17:37.027 --> 00:17:39.024
for new business and client services

00:17:39.024 --> 00:17:41.345
and all the normal things
one has to worry about.

00:17:41.345 --> 00:17:44.247
Now I'm the leader, now this
client has to respect me.

00:17:44.247 --> 00:17:45.995
I need to make Steve happy.

00:17:45.995 --> 00:17:47.094
I don't care about that.

00:17:47.094 --> 00:17:48.729
I can take care of that
at the highest levels.

00:17:48.729 --> 00:17:52.072
It's about really empowering
people to become leaders.

00:17:52.072 --> 00:17:55.531
And leaders, you know I love this.

00:17:55.531 --> 00:17:57.830
This is obviously a subject matter I love.

00:17:57.830 --> 00:18:00.178
We talked about it even
in yesterday's episode.

00:18:00.178 --> 00:18:03.867
It takes so much more motherly,

00:18:03.867 --> 00:18:07.430
historically, stereotypically
motherly skills

00:18:07.430 --> 00:18:09.625
to be a leader and I
think people are confused.

00:18:09.625 --> 00:18:12.248
I think by default people
think it's fatherly stuff

00:18:12.248 --> 00:18:13.629
and I think it's motherly stuff.

00:18:13.629 --> 00:18:16.544
It's emotional skills that allow

00:18:16.544 --> 00:18:18.285
somebody to make that transition.

00:18:18.285 --> 00:18:20.827
And really one of the biggest factors

00:18:20.827 --> 00:18:23.749
in this whole thing is self esteem.

00:18:23.749 --> 00:18:26.028
If you're not able to believe in yourself

00:18:26.028 --> 00:18:27.886
nobody else is gonna believe in you.

00:18:27.886 --> 00:18:29.766
So I think one of the things is,

00:18:29.766 --> 00:18:32.564
look I got fortunate, I
got a mother that instilled

00:18:32.564 --> 00:18:34.445
so much self esteem in me that I'm still

00:18:34.445 --> 00:18:35.988
trying to get some of it out of me

00:18:35.988 --> 00:18:40.177
so that I don't come across
as an egotistical crap head.

00:18:40.177 --> 00:18:41.944
But I think a lot of
people don't have that.

00:18:41.944 --> 00:18:45.491
A lot of people in my family
don't have that, and I see it

00:18:45.491 --> 00:18:47.761
and one of the weird
little tidbits that I think

00:18:47.761 --> 00:18:49.327
can make this episode valuable is

00:18:49.327 --> 00:18:51.707
if you get into a leadership spot

00:18:51.707 --> 00:18:53.356
and if your self aware enough to know that

00:18:53.356 --> 00:18:54.977
your mom or dad put you
down your whole life,

00:18:54.977 --> 00:18:58.000
or society did, or you
grew up as a minority,

00:18:58.000 --> 00:19:00.484
or whatever took self
esteem out of your body.

00:19:00.484 --> 00:19:02.365
Or if you never instilled
in the first place.

00:19:02.365 --> 00:19:06.153
I think you need to find
an outlet to create it.

00:19:06.153 --> 00:19:08.863
I think you need to find
an outlet to create it.

00:19:08.863 --> 00:19:12.884
I think one of the things I
focus on here is I instill it.

00:19:12.884 --> 00:19:14.715
You know, I instill it.

00:19:14.715 --> 00:19:17.176
I do talk 90% of the
time about the positives.

00:19:17.176 --> 00:19:18.279
I just can't help it.

00:19:18.279 --> 00:19:20.460
I'm optimistic and I just see it.

00:19:20.460 --> 00:19:22.340
I see the good, it's what I do.

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:25.336
There's always bad, but I
think you need to seek it out.

00:19:25.336 --> 00:19:28.354
And it might come in the form
of extracurricular activities.

00:19:28.354 --> 00:19:30.793
You might be a great soccer
player, or an improv actor.

00:19:30.793 --> 00:19:33.231
Or maybe the person you date,

00:19:33.231 --> 00:19:36.121
maybe you look for somebody
who, instills that.

00:19:36.121 --> 00:19:38.570
I think that's a very
attractive characteristic.

00:19:38.570 --> 00:19:40.798
I can tell you the reason
I married Lizzie so quickly

00:19:40.798 --> 00:19:42.738
was 'cause she was my mom.

00:19:42.738 --> 00:19:44.538
And so I like coming home
and having a cheerleader.

00:19:44.538 --> 00:19:45.339
Like, you're great.

00:19:45.339 --> 00:19:46.778
I love that. I want that.

00:19:46.778 --> 00:19:50.517
And so, I think those are
